    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1A449B35167BB40706C055A54E4FB0DA/jobs/461213": {
      "title": "Program Assistant",
      "description": "Program Assistant (46466) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Program Assistant (46466) Hybrid   •   San Francisco - San Francisco, CA 94105 Apply Overview Salary Range $19.61 - $19.61 Hourly Position Type Part Time Apply Description Position Title: Program Assistant Department Name: GGU Worldwide Position Reports To: Associate Director of Administration and Partner Support, GGU Worldwide FLSA STATUS : Hourly, Non-Exempt, Part-time Hourly Rate: $19.61 Benefits: Limited University's benefits Days & Hours: Part-time – 20 - 25 hours per week. Variable weekly schedule to be established by supervisor. Flexible schedule may require evening and weekend hours during peak periods. Summary: Provide administrative, operational, research, and analytics support to GGU Worldwide and Beacon Education (China) partnership. This position will include training in our CRM system, Teams, Outlook, myGGU, and Colleague. Assist with other projects and administrative tasks as needed. Essential Duties and Responsibilities : Support Beacon Education (China) partnership, including pre-admission, enrollment, verification, course enrollment, attendance monitoring, WASC reports, translation/interpretation, and academic quality initiatives. Manage data and documentation effectively, including proper use of OneDrive for secure storage and access control. Ensure use of standardized formats for student information and documents and follow GGU IT protocols when sharing files externally. Assist with graduation logistics, including booth setup, regalia distribution, registration flow, and pre-graduation preparations. Maintain a shared calendar to support timely coordination of immersion and graduation activities. Provide regular follow-ups and updates to the Program Coordinator (PC) and Beacon representatives. Generate Beacon graduation invitation and visa support letters. Assist Beacon students and GGU Graduation and Commencement team during campus visits and at commencement site. Beacon event organization and support (virtual, on campus, and off campus). Organize and provide on campus, off campus, study tours and immersions. Coordinate and liaise with International Admissions and Enrollment, Division of Student Success, International Student Services, other GGU schools, and international student groups and organizations. Network and coordinate with San Francisco’s Chinese American community and companies. Adapt to varying schedules and time zones, including evenings and weekends. Other administrative and research duties and responsibilities as required. Qualifications Qualifications, Education and/or Experiences: Knowledge of: General office procedures, MS Office, ChatGPT. Global messaging apps including but not limited to WeChat, Viber, WhatsApp, Messenger. Understand what constitutes professional behavior and good teamwork. Ability to: Communicate clearly and respectfully in written and verbal English and Chinese (Mandarin), organize information, attention to detail and work independently and be creative. Work outside of normal business hours and time zones. Be positive, patient, caring, optimistic, and have a can-do attitude. Language proficiency in Chinese (Mandarin) is required. Experience and Education: Previous office management and operations experience, along with basic computer, internet, and social media skills. Previous international education or organization experience is preferred. A bachelor’s degree in business, public administration, political science, education, international studies, psychology, development studies, economics, Chinese Studies, or other related fields is preferred. Supplemental Information To apply: Please go to http://www.ggu.edu/jobs to submit an application, letter of interest, and a resume. In applying for a position, candidates sign a consent authorizing a broader inquiry which may include reference checks, a motor vehicle check, and a third-party background check. Golden Gate University is an equal employment opportunity employer. We support inclusive excellence and are committed to creating a safe and welcoming community for all. University policy prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion/creed, national origin, ancestry, age, sex/gender, sexual orientation, marital status, medical condition, physical or mental disability, gender stereotyping, and gender identity, gender expression, military or veteran status, taking a protected leave (e.g., family medical or pregnancy leave), or on any other basis protected by applicable laws. The University has a strong commitment to the principles of diversity and inclusion, and to maintaining working and learning environments that reinforces these practices. The University welcomes and encourages applications from all demographics. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1A449B35167BB40706C055A54E4FB0DA/jobs/461190": {
      "title": "Position Summary",
      "description": "Vice President of Finance and Deputy Chief Financial Officer (46494) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Vice President of Finance and Deputy Chief Financial Officer (46494) Fully Remote   •   San Francisco - San Francisco, CA 94105 Apply Overview Salary Range $275,000.00 - $310,000.00 Salary/year Position Type Full Time Apply Description Golden Gate University Position Description Position Title: Vice President of Finance and Deputy Chief Financial Officer Department: Finance and Administration Reports To: Senior Vice President of Operations and Chief Financial Officer Classification: Full-Time, Exempt Location: Remote, with periodic travel to San Francisco Salary Range: $275,000 - $310,000 annually, commensurate with experience About Golden Gate University Golden Gate University is a private, nonprofit university in San Francisco focused on AI and built for working adults. The University serves a diverse community of students across its academic programs and is committed to providing an accessible, high-quality education for working adults. Position Summary The Vice President of Finance and Deputy Chief Financial Officer is the senior-most finance leader below the Chief Financial Officer and serves as the operating leader of the University’s finance function. The position carries direct responsibility for accounting and financial reporting, budgeting and financial planning, treasury and cash management, and the day-to-day business operations that support students, faculty, and staff. The Vice President partners closely with the Chief Financial Officer on the University’s multi-year financial strategy, capital structure, and Board reporting, and is expected to act with the full authority of the Chief Financial Officer in his or her absence. The role is well suited to a candidate who is equally comfortable building a multi-year financial model, closing the books, and explaining the resulting picture to a dean, a lender, or a trustee. The position reports to the Senior Vice President of Operations and Chief Financial Officer and leads a team spanning accounting, financial planning and analysis, student accounts, and procurement. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Financial Planning, Budgeting, and Analysis Lead the annual operating and capital budget process, including calendar, guidance, unit-level submissions, and presentation of the recommended budget to senior leadership and the Board. Own the University’s multi-year financial plan and scenario models, translating enrollment, pricing, discounting, and program assumptions into net tuition revenue, margin, and cash outcomes. Produce monthly and quarterly management reporting that compares results to budget and forecast, explains variances in operating terms, and identifies corrective action early enough to matter. Partner with Enrollment Management, Academic Affairs, and Marketing on program-level contribution analysis, pricing and discount strategy, and the business case for new and sunsetting programs. Support the design and administration of the University’s budget model and the allocation methodologies that connect unit performance to resources. Accounting, Controllership, and Financial Reporting Oversee all accounting operations, including the general ledger, monthly and year-end close, revenue recognition, grants and restricted funds, gift and endowment accounting, fixed assets, payroll accounting, accounts payable, and accounts receivable. Maintain a system of internal controls appropriate to the University’s size and risk profile, and lead management responses to internal audit and external audit findings through to closure. Oversee tax matters, including unrelated business income, sales and use tax, information reporting, and the accounting and tax treatment of the University’s subsidiaries and international operations. Drive continuous improvement of finance systems, the chart of accounts, and reporting infrastructure, including automation and AI-enabled workflows that reduce manual effort and shorten the close. Treasury, Debt, and Capital Manage cash and liquidity, including short-term forecasting, working capital management, banking relationships, credit facilities, and covenant compliance and reporting. Support the University’s capital structure and financing activity, including debt issuance and refinancing, lender and bond counsel coordination, continuing disclosure obligations, and rating agency materials and relationships. Coordinate endowment and investment reporting with the University’s investment advisors and the relevant Board committee, including spending policy calculations and donor-restricted fund compliance. Provide financial analysis for real estate, facilities, and technology investments, including lease-versus-buy analysis, total cost of ownership, and post-implementation review of results against the approved case. Business Operations Oversee student financial services, including tuition billing, payment plans, refunds, collections, and the reconciliation of student accounts with financial aid disbursements and Title IV requirements. Oversee procurement and contracting operations, including purchasing policy, vendor management, and coordination with the Office of the General Counsel on contract review and risk allocation. Develop and maintain financial policies and procedures, and deliver training that helps budget managers across the University understand and meet their stewardship responsibilities. Leadership and Governance Lead, develop, and hold accountable a distributed finance team, setting clear standards for accuracy, responsiveness, and service to the University community. Prepare materials for and present to the Finance, Audit, and Investment Committees of the Board of Trustees, and serve as a credible institutional voice with trustees, lenders, auditors, and regulators. Serve as acting Chief Financial Officer in the Chief Financial Officer’s absence, and represent the finance function on cross-functional institutional initiatives. Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree in accounting, finance, economics, or a related field. Ten or more years of progressively responsible financial leadership experience, including direct responsibility for both accounting operations and budgeting or financial planning. Working command of U.S. GAAP for not-for-profit organizations and demonstrated experience owning or materially supporting an external audit. Demonstrated ability to build financial models and multi-year forecasts from source data, and to explain the assumptions and results to audiences without a finance background. Experience leading and developing a finance team, including hiring, performance management, and process redesign. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, sound judgment, and the ability to manage competing priorities independently in a remote environment. Preferred CPA, MBA, or equivalent advanced credential. Experience in higher education finance, including net tuition revenue analysis, financial aid and Title IV interaction, budget model design, and Board committee reporting. Experience with tax-exempt debt, lender or rating agency relationships, and covenant compliance. Experience with enterprise finance and student information systems, and with automation or AI-enabled improvements to finance operations. Public accounting or advisory experience serving nonprofit or higher education clients. Supplemental Information To apply: Please go to http://www.ggu.edu/jobs to submit an application, letter of interest, and a resume. In applying for a position, candidates sign a consent authorizing a broader inquiry which may include reference checks, a motor vehicle check, and a third-party background check. Golden Gate University is an equal employment opportunity employer. We support inclusive excellence and are committed to creating a safe and welcoming community for all. University policy prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion/creed, national origin, ancestry, age, sex/gender, sexual orientation, marital status, medical condition, physical or mental disability, gender stereotyping, and gender identity, gender expression, military or veteran status, taking a protected leave (e.g., family medical or pregnancy leave), or on any other basis protected by applicable laws. The University has a strong commitment to the principles of diversity and inclusion, and to maintaining working and learning environments that reinforces these practices. The University welcomes and encourages applications from all demographics. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1A449B35167BB40706C055A54E4FB0DA/jobs/463533": {
      "title": "AI Engineer",
      "description": "AI Engineer (46606) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account AI Engineer (46606) Fully Remote   •   San Francisco - San Francisco, CA 94105 Apply Overview Salary Range $150,000.00 - $175,000.00 Salary/year Apply Description Golden Gate University Position Description Position Title: AI Engineer Department: Office of the President Reports To: Chief of Staff Classification: Full-Time, Exempt Location: Remote About Golden Gate University Golden Gate University is a private, nonprofit university in San Francisco focused on AI and built for working adults. GGU has served working adults for more than 120 years, and the University is building its future around AI, in its academic programs and in how the institution itself runs. This position is central to the second part. Position Summary The AI Engineer supports the redesign of GGU’s data infrastructure and reporting functions, with AI at the core, in partnership with the University’s executive leadership team. The role has three core responsibilities. First, build the AI tools and workflows that make GGU’s reporting functions more effective and efficient, including required reports to outside agencies and internal reports for University leadership. Second, build AI tools for data analysis in areas of leadership priority, such as enrollment projections and financial projections. Third, design AI tools and workflows to support other leadership priorities as assigned, in coordination with the Vice Provost for Digital Innovation where the work touches the academic experience or shared platforms. The position reports to the President’s Chief of Staff. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Data Reporting Tools and Workflows Support the redesign of the University’s data infrastructure and reporting functions, with AI at the core, in partnership with executive leadership. Build AI tools and workflows to automate data flows from the University’s student information system (Ellucian Colleague) and other core systems. Make the University’s reports faster to produce and more reliable, including required reports to outside agencies and internal reports for leadership. Own the AI stack for institutional reporting and analysis: choose models and platforms, design prompts and agents, test for reliability, and manage integrations and costs. Model, platform, and vendor choices that overlap with the academic technology stack are made in coordination with the Vice Provost for Digital Innovation, who holds technology strategy and vendor management for the academic experience. Keep the University’s data safe, with FERPA compliance, proper access controls, and good data governance, coordinating with the University’s outsourced IT partner on system access and infrastructure and with GGU Digital where data governance touches academic systems. Data Analysis Tools Build AI tools for analysis in areas of leadership priority, such as enrollment projections and financial projections. Work from goals and methods framed by the leadership team, and deliver tools that work and keep working. Turn analyses framed in spreadsheets into durable, self-updating tools. Other Leadership Priorities Design AI tools and workflows in other areas of the University as needs arise, coordinating with the Vice Provost for Digital Innovation on any work that extends into the academic experience. Advise leadership on what AI tools can and cannot do well in institutional operations, and recommend where they will help most. Qualifications Required Demonstrated experience building LLM-based tools used in production, including agent workflows, retrieval, structured extraction, tool use, and evaluation. Strong Python and solid SQL, with enough data engineering experience to connect AI tools to institutional systems reliably. A record of AI tools built, shipped, and maintained. Candidates should be prepared to demonstrate one. The ability to take a well-framed request from a non-technical leader and return a working tool, independently. Excellent communication skills and the ability to work effectively with senior leadership. Preferred Experience in higher education. Experience with Ellucian Colleague or another student information system. Familiarity with FERPA. Compensation The salary range for this position is $150,000 to $175,000 depending on experience. Application Process Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest and resume to Nicholas Bradley at nbradley@ggu.edu. Candidates are encouraged to include a short note about an AI tool they built: what it does, who uses it, and how it has held up. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Supplemental Information To apply: Please go to http://www.ggu.edu/jobs to submit an application, letter of interest, and a resume. In applying for a position, candidates sign a consent authorizing a broader inquiry which may include reference checks, a motor vehicle check, and a third-party background check. Golden Gate University is an equal employment opportunity employer. We support inclusive excellence and are committed to creating a safe and welcoming community for all. University policy prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion/creed, national origin, ancestry, age, sex/gender, sexual orientation, marital status, medical condition, physical or mental disability, gender stereotyping, and gender identity, gender expression, military or veteran status, taking a protected leave (e.g., family medical or pregnancy leave), or on any other basis protected by applicable laws. The University has a strong commitment to the principles of diversity and inclusion, and to maintaining working and learning environments that reinforces these practices. The University welcomes and encourages applications from all demographics. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1A449B35167BB40706C055A54E4FB0DA/jobs/463558": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, School of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, School of Psychology (46605) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, School of Psychology (46605) Fully Remote   •   San Francisco - San Francisco, CA 94105 Apply Overview Salary Range $95,000.00 - $95,000.00 Salary/year Apply Description Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track Faculty Position School of Psychology · Golden Gate University · San Francisco, CA Position Overview The School of Psychology at Golden Gate University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position. The successful candidate will join a growing School that prepares the next generation of practitioners and applied scholars across counseling psychology, marriage and family therapy, and industrial-organizational psychology, with an emerging focus on human systems in the age of AI. The position combines teaching, program leadership, scholarship, and service. The faculty member will play a central role in the day-to-day operation, regulatory integrity, external partnerships, and strategic direction of the School's degree programs. Reports to: Founding Dean, School of Psychology Appointment: Full-time, tenure-track (rank commensurate with experience) Location: Golden Gate University, San Francisco campus, with hybrid and online responsibilities and occasional domestic and international travel Essential Responsibilities Teaching Carry a full-time teaching load across the School's graduate programs in counseling psychology, marriage and family therapy, and industrial-organizational psychology, with teaching modalities spanning in-person, hybrid (HSF), and fully online formats. Develop, revise, and maintain course materials, syllabi, rubrics, and assessment instruments aligned with program learning outcomes and accreditation standards. Mentor and advise students academically and professionally, including support for clinical development, research, and career planning. Counseling Psychology Program Leadership and Clinical Training Provide oversight and ongoing maintenance of the clinical training program within the MA Counseling Psychology degree, including practicum and internship structures, training curriculum, and student progress monitoring. Monitor and ensure program compliance with licensure laws and regulations across all states in which the program operates and in which students seek licensure, including California, and adjust program requirements as state regulations evolve. Maintain accurate, up-to-date documentation of program requirements, clinical training policies, supervision standards, and student-facing materials. Develop, draft, and update licensure disclosures for the program website, university catalog, and other public-facing materials in compliance with state authorization, NC-SARA, and accreditation requirements. Build and maintain relationships with practicum and internship sites throughout the Bay Area and across the United States, including conducting outreach, negotiating affiliation agreements, supporting site supervisors, and resolving placement concerns. Industrial-Organizational Psychology Program Support Contribute to the continued development and expansion of the I-O Psychology internship program, including identifying new partner organizations, designing internship structures, and supporting student placement. Collaborate on operating procedures, documentation, and policies that serve both the Counseling Psychology and I-O Psychology programs, ensuring alignment with School-wide standards and institutional policy. International Partnerships Maintain ongoing communication and collaboration with the School's international partners. Represent the School of Psychology at international partner sites through occasional travel, including teaching residencies, partner meetings, and recruitment or program-development engagements. Program Operations and Events Coordinate and plan academic camps, intensive weeks, weekend residencies, and other events that anchor the School's hybrid and low-residency program offerings. Assist with class scheduling each term, balancing program needs, modality requirements, and faculty workload. Assist with recruitment, vetting, onboarding, and ongoing support of adjunct faculty, in coordination with program directors and the Dean. Scholarship and Professional Activity Maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to rank, including peer-reviewed publication, applied research, public scholarship, or other forms of intellectual contribution recognized in the candidate's field. Contribute to the School's developing research agenda on human systems in the age of AI, with opportunities to participate in the Human Systems Lab and related initiatives. Service Serve on the Trainee Candidacy Committee, reviewing student progression through clinical training milestones. Represent Golden Gate University at, and co-chair, the Bay Area MFT Educator Consortium. Represent the Bay Area MFT Educator Consortium at the statewide California MFT Leadership Collaborative. Participate in virtual and Campus Week programming and Psychology Club meetings. Serve on additional School, University, and program-level committees, including admissions, curriculum, and accreditation work. Participate in faculty governance and contribute to the strategic development of the School of Psychology. Engage in professional service to the field through editorial work, conference participation, professional association leadership, or related activities. Program Vision and Strategic Priorities The successful candidate will help advance the following strategic priorities for the School's degree programs: Align curricular and training requirements with COAMFTE and CACREP equivalency to improve the portability of degrees and maximize licensure pathways for graduates. Prepare learners for AI-supported legal and ethical clinical practice in a rapidly changing professional landscape. Modernize assessment to include a biopsychosocialtechnological lens that incorporates human systems–AI relationships. Implement AI tools that support clinical skill development and competency-based learning. Launch doctoral programs that prepare graduates for research, leadership, academic, and program-development roles in the MFT and counseling fields. Qualifications Required Qualifications Doctoral degree (PhD, PsyD, or equivalent) in Counseling Psychology, Clinical Psychology, Marriage and Family Therapy, Industrial-Organizational Psychology, or a closely related field. Eligibility for or current licensure as a psychologist, LMFT, LPCC, or related credential, as appropriate to the program focus. Demonstrated experience in graduate-level teaching, clinical supervision, or applied training. Working knowledge of state-level licensure regulations affecting graduate-level mental health training programs, and a willingness to develop expertise in multi-state regulatory compliance. Strong administrative, organizational, and documentation skills. Ability and willingness to travel domestically and internationally as required. Preferred Qualifications Experience overseeing or contributing to clinical training programs, internship placement, or program accreditation, including familiarity with COAMFTE and/or CACREP standards. Familiarity with hybrid and online graduate program delivery, including modalities such as HyFlex or low-residency formats. Established scholarly trajectory and active engagement with public, applied, or interdisciplinary scholarship. Interest in or familiarity with the intersection of psychology, technology, and human development, including AI-supported clinical training and practice. Experience working with international academic partners and culturally diverse student populations. Experience contributing to doctoral program design or doctoral-level teaching and mentorship. Supplemental Information To apply: Please go to http://www.ggu.edu/jobs to submit an application, letter of interest, and a resume. In applying for a position, candidates sign a consent authorizing a broader inquiry which may include reference checks, a motor vehicle check, and a third-party background check. Golden Gate University is an equal employment opportunity employer. We support inclusive excellence and are committed to creating a safe and welcoming community for all. University policy prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion/creed, national origin, ancestry, age, sex/gender, sexual orientation, marital status, medical condition, physical or mental disability, gender stereotyping, and gender identity, gender expression, military or veteran status, taking a protected leave (e.g., family medical or pregnancy leave), or on any other basis protected by applicable laws. The University has a strong commitment to the principles of diversity and inclusion, and to maintaining working and learning environments that reinforces these practices. The University welcomes and encourages applications from all demographics. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1A449B35167BB40706C055A54E4FB0DA/jobs/463703": {
      "title": "Director of Learning Design, Assessment, and Emerging Technology",
      "description": "Director of Learning Design, Assessment, and Emerging Technology (46616) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Director of Learning Design, Assessment, and Emerging Technology (46616) Fully Remote   •   San Francisco - San Francisco, CA 94105 Apply Overview Salary Range $140,000.00 - $140,000.00 Salary/year Apply Description Director of Learning Design, Assessment, and Emerging Technology Digital Teaching, Learning, and Development | Golden Gate University Job summary TDirector of Learning Design, Assessment, and Emerging Technology leads the learning design and assessment function for the university’s online, hybrid and AI-enabled courses across every school and program, reporting to the Vice Provost for Digital Innovation. The Director is responsible for the standards courses are held to, the readiness review every course passes before students see it, the validity and reliability of course assessment, and the design methodology behind the university’s course model. The Director manages a team of instructional designers and learning technologists, directs their work against the university’s course development process, and advises programs and academic leadership on curriculum, evaluation and faculty development. The ideal candidate is an experienced learning designer and people manager with formal training in assessment and measurement, a record of quality assurance at program scale, experience working with external development partners, and sound judgment about where generative AI should and should not be used in teaching and learning. Responsibilities Course quality, design and readiness (35%) Define the quality standard for digital courses: content accuracy, alignment of outcomes, activities and assessments, assignment and rubric quality, and accessibility. Maintain the instruments that test for it and report conformance. Lead launch readiness: the standard, the pre-launch review, and the determination that a course is ready for students, with problems identified early and a remedy or hold recommended. Lead design delivery: the course design documentation behind each course, the work of the design staff against it, and acceptance of developed courses against specification. Serve as the primary design liaison to programs and faculty, and carry a personal design load on complex, novel and research-driven courses. Contribute to the institutional course standard and the design methodology behind it, and report what implementation shows about where a standard is not working. Team and standards (25%) Recruit, hire, direct, develop and evaluate the learning design and technology staff, and set clear ownership for each role. Carry the design and quality standards into practice: write the review instruments and guides, train the staff who apply them, and report conformance. Standards cover regular and substantive interaction, accessibility, assessment design, AI use and disclosure, and the evidence retained for accreditation. Direct the team against the course development process, allocate design capacity, specify contracted work and review deliverables, and raise capacity or schedule risk early. Partner with the registrar, accessibility services, the library, information technology and student services on the requirements that reach courses. Manage the department’s professional development and learning technology budget within approved allocations. Assessment quality and measurement (20%) Lead the university’s approach to assuring the validity and reliability of course assessment, including assessment scored with automated and AI-assisted tools. Set the standards for assessment design, rubric quality and scoring consistency. Apply educational measurement methods, including rubric calibration, inter-rater reliability, item analysis and monitoring for drift; design the measurement and interpret the results, with analyses run by learning technology staff. Set the responsible-use requirements for AI in assessment, and produce the assessment evidence relied on for accreditation, coordinating with the Director of Assessment and Evaluation on program-level outcomes. Research, evaluation and learning technology (10%) Investigate open design questions in the course model and deliver options with recommendations for the Vice Provost’s decision. Evaluate what courses produce: outcomes, grade and completion patterns, engagement and student experience. Run experiments in live courses and report findings. Conduct applied research on AI-enabled learning with the Vice Provost and external partners toward publication, support faculty research on their own teaching, and represent the university’s work externally with institutional endorsement. Evaluate emerging tools against the course model and recommend adoption; maintain the department’s AI-assisted design tools and faculty guidance. Academic consultation (10%) Advise programs, deans and academic leadership on curriculum and program design, program revision, and outcomes mapping. Lead course and program evaluation for academic partners, including formative student feedback during a term. Maintain intake and prioritization so requests are scoped and sequenced against production commitments, with anything that would move a production date raised before it is accepted. Qualifications Required Master’s degree in instructional design, education, educational measurement, learning science or a related field. Seven or more years in instructional or learning design in higher education, including three or more supervising designers or learning technologists. Demonstrated expertise in course design and instructional design models, learning management systems, and course design documentation at program scale. Experience with assessment design, rubric development and the validation of scoring, including inter-rater reliability. Experience applying generative AI to course design, assessment or faculty support, with sound judgment about its limits. Working knowledge of accessibility standards, regular and substantive interaction requirements, student privacy law, and accreditation expectations for learning outcomes evidence. Experience teaching online or hybrid courses. Experience setting standards that others apply, and reporting on quality to academic leadership. Preferred Graduate training in measurement, evaluation or research methods. Experience building programs with an external education partner, including setting the standards the partner must meet. Learning analytics experience; Quality Matters, OLC or comparable credentials; a record of conference presentation or publication. Additional information Full-time, exempt. Remote. Reports to the Vice Provost for Digital Innovation. Direct reports as staffed. Compensation commensurate with experience at the director band. Golden Gate University is an equal opportunity employer. Supplemental Information To apply: Please go to http://www.ggu.edu/jobs to submit an application, letter of interest, and a resume. In applying for a position, candidates sign a consent authorizing a broader inquiry which may include reference checks, a motor vehicle check, and a third-party background check. Golden Gate University is an equal employment opportunity employer. We support inclusive excellence and are committed to creating a safe and welcoming community for all. University policy prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion/creed, national origin, ancestry, age, sex/gender, sexual orientation, marital status, medical condition, physical or mental disability, gender stereotyping, and gender identity, gender expression, military or veteran status, taking a protected leave (e.g., family medical or pregnancy leave), or on any other basis protected by applicable laws. The University has a strong commitment to the principles of diversity and inclusion, and to maintaining working and learning environments that reinforces these practices. The University welcomes and encourages applications from all demographics. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/1A449B35167BB40706C055A54E4FB0DA/jobs/464051": {
      "title": "School Certifying Official (SCO)",
      "description": "School Certifying Official (SCO) (46618) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account School Certifying Official (SCO) (46618) Fully Remote   •   San Francisco - San Francisco, CA 94105 Apply Overview Salary Range $70,304.00 - $75,000.00 Salary/year Apply Description School Certifying Official (SCO) Job Title: School Certifying Official Reports To: Associate Director of Student Financial Services Department: Student Financial Services Salary: $70,304 - $75,000 Location: Remote Position Summary Under the direction of and reporting to the Associate Director, a School Certifying Official (SCO) works with our team of SCOs to ensures GGU complies with U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) regulations and process enrollment certifications, monitor academic progress, and act as a critical liaison between student veterans and the VA to guarantee uninterrupted educational benefits. Each SCO is responsible for a designated cohort of students Key Responsibilities Benefit Certification: Evaluate student schedules and degree audits, then accurately submit enrollment certifications to the VA via their digital platforms. Compliance & Auditing: Maintain deep familiarity with all VA education benefit chapters (e.g., Post-9/11 GI Bill, Montgomery GI Bill, VR&E) to ensure institutional compliance and prevent financial overpayments. Student Advising: Counsel student veterans on benefit eligibility, required paperwork, and military tuition assistance. Academic Monitoring: Track the academic progress, enrollment changes, and graduation status of VA-funded students, promptly reporting any adjustments to the VA. Cross-Department Collaboration: Partner with Financial Aid, Student Accounts, and Academic Advising to ensure student files are complete and institutional reporting standards are met. Qualifications Qualifications & Skills Experience and Education: Bachelor’s degree, minimum of 2–3 years of experience in higher education, preferably within a Registrar's Office, Financial Aid, or Veterans Affairs department or a combination of education and related experience. Compliance Knowledge: Direct, hands-on experience using VA-ONCE or the modern VA Enrollment Manager system is strongly preferred. Communication: Exceptional organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills to guide non-traditional students and manage confidential data securely. Service Excellence: Dedicated to delivering high-quality, responsive customer service to the university community. Workload: High-volume periods occur prior to and during the start of academic terms (e.g., January, April/May and August/September). Supplemental Information To apply: Please go to http://www.ggu.edu/jobs to submit an application, letter of interest, and a resume. In applying for a position, candidates sign a consent authorizing a broader inquiry which may include reference checks, a motor vehicle check, and a third-party background check. Golden Gate University is an equal employment opportunity employer. We support inclusive excellence and are committed to creating a safe and welcoming community for all. University policy prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion/creed, national origin, ancestry, age, sex/gender, sexual orientation, marital status, medical condition, physical or mental disability, gender stereotyping, and gender identity, gender expression, military or veteran status, taking a protected leave (e.g., family medical or pregnancy leave), or on any other basis protected by applicable laws. The University has a strong commitment to the principles of diversity and inclusion, and to maintaining working and learning environments that reinforces these practices. The University welcomes and encourages applications from all demographics. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
